00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.389 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  this morning we remember charlie watts  the legendary rolling stones drummer who  stayed out of the spotlight that  followed the band everywhere watts died  yesterday at a london hospital several  weeks after an unspecified medical  procedure he was 80 years old  mick jagger honored his band mate of  nearly six decades tweeting a photo of  watts with a big smile keith richards  posted an empty drum set with a sign  reading closed here's a look back at  what's brilliant career  in a band known for its brash  personality charlie watts was its quiet  backbone  for more than half a century his steady  beat anchored the rolling stones which  he formed alongside mick jagger keith  richards bill wyman and the late brian  jones watts spoke to 60 minutes ed  bradley in 1994. and i always consider  myself a drummer you know and uh  so that's to keep the time and  help everybody else do what they do you  know  i don't really like  solo type things  his drumming skills self-taught by  listening to jazz greats like charlie  parker and duke ellington it's one of  the faults in my playing because i never  learned to play  the stones had searched for a drummer  for months before finding watts and  feared he might be too talented for them  but he ended up playing on all 30  rolling stones albums and on every tour  despite his sharp fashion sense he shied  away from flashy drum solos and the  attention that came with international  super stardom watts defied all the  stereotypes that came with being in  arguably the world's most influential  rock band while jagger and richards  mugged for the camera he was shy and  introverted as drugs ravaged the era's  bands including the stones watts  indulged but then quit cold turkey  he married his wife shirley in 1964  and they stayed together and raised a  family  [Music]  [Applause]  watt's passing comes about three weeks  after he pulled out of the band's  upcoming tour citing health reasons  i knew he was ill but i didn't know  it was this beatles legend paul  mccartney paid tribute in this video  message condolences to the stones should  be a huge blow to them because  charlie was a rock man of fantastic  drama  [Applause]  and from what i understand even though  he was ill and and had been replaced for  the upcoming tour uh this came as a  surprise to the rolling stones too we  still don't know exactly we don't know  what happened we don't know what  happened he seemed to have such a low  key kind of cool i like when he said i  don't like solo type things right he's  one of the biggest bands in the world he  said at one point i wasn't interested in  being a pop idol and i still think it's  silly  yeah i mean he went on tour with the  rolling stones for every tour and you  think how much fun would that be well in  the 90s he said every night he went back  to his hotel room alone and drew his bed  he has a sketch of every bed he ever  slept on for the entire run i love that  i love it that's a different kind of  rock a different kind of rock star  dignified i mean keith richards wrote in  his book he said charlie watts has  always been the bed eye lion musically  and that's he really was the backbone of  the band i really do like the shot of  the drums with the sign that just says  closed i think it's really poignant and  really tells the story no it really  startled me when that came across  yesterday one other fun fact about how  times change when they were trying to  get charlie watts they thought they  couldn't afford him right because he  wanted five dollars a week five pounds i  think five pounds yeah yeah which seemed  like a like a fortune keith richard said  they they actually shoplifted so they  could raise the money i don't think he  was kidding about that

